;W[nf];B[ng]C[Hendl [1d\]\: amashi is to let the opponent take big points and outlast him at the end
Hobbes [1k\]\: c'mon hendl
Muttley [2k\]\: From sensei's\: http\://senseis.xmp.net/?Amashi
Muttley [2k\]\: I'm right \:)
Hobbes [1k\]\: ofc
Muttley [2k\]\: The aim of amashi is to outlast the opponent, to let him take good points, but to follow hard on his heels by taking profit. Shuwa refined amashi to a very high level as a strategy for White in no-komi games. The natural tendency with amashi is for the game to become a contest between Black's thickness and White's profit.
Muttley [2k\]\: The key is, taking profit, which in the end wins the game
tadanobu [6k\]\: but you shouldn't try to "survive everything"... just what is essential
Muttley [2k\]\: sure, it is a complex, subtle strategy, but it is still "give it your best shot, do your worst, I've got points" strategy
Hendl [1d\]\: \:-)
Y [-\]\: Muttley is right. 
TurtleDove [4k\]\: does the scribe get a vote on whether it's time for lunch ? ;)
Muttley [2k\]\: Sensei's page is very good in this respect, describing it as a cascading mastery of several techniques
tadanobu [6k\]\: agreed
Hobbes [1k\]\: shinogi being one of them, I think
Hendl [1d\]\: if one group´s life at stake in your points taking, its shinogi strategy
Muttley [2k\]\: clearly, here b let w take "big points" and "dangled the weak groups", whilst taking more territory
Verse [2k\]\: was this an even game?
Muttley [2k\]\: of course
Muttley [2k\]\: it is in the EGC
